            <ArticleTitle>Transdermal oxycodone patch for the treatment of postherpetic neuralgia: a randomized, double-blind, controlled trial.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>255-267</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2217/pmt-2016-0067</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the efficacy, systemic exposure, safety and tolerability of a transdermal oxycodone patch containing tocopheryl phosphate mixture (TPM) in patients with postherpetic neuralgia (PHN).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS &amp; METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The study was a Phase IIa, multicenter, randomized, double-blind, vehicle-controlled crossover study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">While the TPM/oxycodone patch did not significantly improve 'average' Numeric Pain Rating Scale scores versus vehicle patch, patients reporting high levels of paresthesia (n = 9) showed a trend toward improved pain reduction. The TPM/oxycodone patch resulted in a low systemic exposure to oxycodone and was well tolerated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The TPM/oxycodone patch delivered oxycodone to the site of perceived pain in subjects suffering from PHN, but did not provide analgesia for the broad PHN indication.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
